#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053e2508-4f71-5213-b86a-ba83adb19b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 y e^{- 2 x y} \\cos{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ - 2 x e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ 2 z e^{- 2 x y} \\cos{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705561} {:answer/id #uuid "183b9880-7e0f-585d-82e3-89fe7522df22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 y e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ - 2 x e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ 2 z e^{- 2 x y} \\cos{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705562} {:answer/id #uuid "3d20bc6a-a816-56f3-8c53-902370c47e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 y e^{- 2 x y} \\cos{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ 2 z e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705565} {:answer/id #uuid "5036def0-e2c0-59d1-b1be-476b373db7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 y e^{- 2 x y} \\cos{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ - 2 x e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ 2 z e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705566} {:answer/id #uuid "63ee8012-54c6-5a01-8004-ab6313a7a46b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 y e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ - 2 x e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ 2 z e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705562} :answer-field/id #uuid "933a20d1-7ab4-5a78-9b94-b8b03da4098e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "03741800-73a8-57a5-acdd-b9cb4584b53d" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ given $f(x,y,z)=e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Hold the other two coordinates constant in each partial derivative. Apply the power, chain, or product rule to the factors that depend on the active coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=- 2 y e^{- 2 y x} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ &=- 2 y e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=- 2 x e^{- 2 y x} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ &=- 2 x e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=2 z e^{- 2 y x} \\cos{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ &=2 z e^{- 2 x y} \\cos{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partials in $x,y,z$ order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 y e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ - 2 x e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ 2 z e^{- 2 x y} \\cos{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eba092b-84eb-5d3d-bafb-8940a42fba56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705568} {:answer/id #uuid "687bd6a3-fafd-52ad-a008-ebaa35c32230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705573} {:answer/id #uuid "8a044c93-23f8-5e8c-b1d0-d29e4728eea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705574} {:answer/id #uuid "a38eab5e-9393-5935-b22f-d32fdfdc3c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705581} {:answer/id #uuid "f033aead-a832-5c72-a878-d273b4763a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705592}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705573} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a8cf7b9-6e34-5da6-a94a-a2703d18c7a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8ce15c9a-6e5a-53d2-abe0-cbf019e52d30" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ at $(2,2,3)$ given $f(x,y,z)=\\ln{\\left(\\sqrt{x z \\left(y - 1\\right)} \\right)}.$ Work where $z>0$, $x>0$, $y - 1>0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other two coordinates constant in each partial derivative. Apply the power, chain, or product rule to the factors that depend on the active coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{z \\left(y - 1\\right) \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z x \\left(y - 1\\right)}}}{\\sqrt{z x \\left(y - 1\\right)}} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2 x} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{z x \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z x \\left(y - 1\\right)}}}{\\sqrt{z x \\left(y - 1\\right)}} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2 \\left(y - 1\\right)} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{x \\left(y - 1\\right) \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z x \\left(y - 1\\right)}}}{\\sqrt{z x \\left(y - 1\\right)}} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2 z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partials in $x,y,z$ order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{2 x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2 \\left(y - 1\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2 z}\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=2$, $y=2$, $z=3$ into these partial derivatives.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(2,2,3)&=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f_{y}(2,2,3)&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f_{z}(2,2,3)&=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(2,2,3)&=\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c3909f-6f4a-5325-bb56-10b23c0f2e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}x z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ x z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ x z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705558} {:answer/id #uuid "65bf19bb-f430-5cf4-a647-f6db529a6a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{x z}{y} \\\\ x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705572} {:answer/id #uuid "90af1b22-5d07-5fef-ad00-a55e9bceb7ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ x z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705579} {:answer/id #uuid "a702aa34-dfa7-5485-9e8d-78d7b177e410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}x z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{x z}{y} \\\\ x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705582} {:answer/id #uuid "ae361ca2-b738-5575-9ca2-9c74152cef58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}x z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ x z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705572} :answer-field/id #uuid "5161db20-867a-5cb4-9c28-bee36fb773f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8674df2-23c5-5fed-aa09-66dfc9a28782" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ given $f(x,y,z)=x z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}.$ Work where $z>0$, $x>0$, $y>0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other two coordinates constant in each partial derivative. Apply the power, chain, or product rule to the factors that depend on the active coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{z x}{y} \\\\ &=\\frac{x z}{y} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partials in $x,y,z$ order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\begin{pmatrix}z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{x z}{y} \\\\ x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3801d9ce-5813-5931-afb7-c97d22715b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705564} {:answer/id #uuid "60e043ae-a2a1-55b1-ab16-220a33caa17d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705569} {:answer/id #uuid "6247baf3-846f-5c5d-ba09-b34c5d1e39af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705570} {:answer/id #uuid "8bec9864-f68d-524f-8535-7fde81d17655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705576} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e63619-5be0-5a83-9a78-d4da288d3286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705587}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705570} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9a8b9d4-149f-5a49-9f47-20c215077e16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cba9efde-e813-5772-b0ac-67e7d68ea6a3" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ at $(-2,2,-1)$ given $f(x,y,z)=- x^{2} \\left(z + 1\\right) + 2 x y + y^{2} \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Hold the other two coordinates constant in each partial derivative. Apply the power, chain, or product rule to the factors that depend on the active coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=- 2 \\left(z + 1\\right) x + 2 y \\\\ &=- 2 x \\left(z + 1\\right) + 2 y \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} y + 2 x \\\\ &=2 x + 2 y \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=2 \\left(z + 1\\right) y^{2} - x^{2} \\\\ &=- x^{2} + 2 y^{2} \\left(z + 1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partials in $x,y,z$ order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 x \\left(z + 1\\right) + 2 y \\\\ 2 x + 2 y \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ - x^{2} + 2 y^{2} \\left(z + 1\\right)\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=-2$, $y=2$, $z=-1$ into these partial derivatives.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(-2,2,-1)&=4 \\\\ f_{y}(-2,2,-1)&=-4 \\\\ f_{z}(-2,2,-1)&=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-2,2,-1)&=\\begin{pmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03061397-dd16-5407-9316-c9c8157e861e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}- \\frac{x}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ - \\frac{y - 1}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{z}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705559} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5cd6b7-403e-5fb3-97cf-9682073ac713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}- \\frac{x}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ - \\frac{y - 1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}}} \\\\ - \\frac{z}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705575} {:answer/id #uuid "b7453579-079d-5c00-8d29-70880a1046e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}- \\frac{x}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ - \\frac{y - 1}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ - \\frac{z}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705586} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ad33b4-0dd6-50ee-b5a7-a0eaed0a0eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}- \\frac{x}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ - \\frac{y - 1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{z}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705589} {:answer/id #uuid "eedca90c-da90-5927-9e9f-19d6c022df00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{pmatrix}- \\frac{1}{2 \\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ - \\frac{y - 1}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{z}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\end{pmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705586} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f06f06c-6bcc-5a6a-91f6-3309dd6495bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dbc2cfc3-8390-5919-8b95-f7fa82b9f8b1" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ given $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}}}.$ Work where $x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} > 0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other two coordinates constant in each partial derivative. Apply the power, chain, or product rule to the factors that depend on the active coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=- \\frac{2 x}{2 \\left(\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + z^{2} + x^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ &=- \\frac{x}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=- \\frac{2 \\left(y - 1\\right)}{2 \\left(\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + z^{2} + x^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ &=- \\frac{y - 1}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=- \\frac{2 z}{2 \\left(\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + z^{2} + x^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ &=- \\frac{z}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partials in $x,y,z$ order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\begin{pmatrix}- \\frac{x}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ - \\frac{y - 1}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ - \\frac{z}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0584bffb-2e7b-5921-8fdc-d16295e00715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705594} {:answer/id #uuid "0c860ec0-794f-5bd9-86c0-43e97b835872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2 \\sqrt{3} + 7}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{- \\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{2 \\sqrt{3} + 7}}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705596} {:answer/id #uuid "76810378-6e5e-5c8f-b51a-1a84273a1a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705615} {:answer/id #uuid "a4141256-83d4-503d-9060-f0d6ca54b2b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705620} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ab196d-a9d5-50ef-885e-ec72b1d08da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{3}}{2 \\sqrt{\\sqrt{3} + 2}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{- \\sqrt{3} - 1}{2 \\sqrt{\\sqrt{3} + 2}}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705624}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705620} :answer-field/id #uuid "58a49492-49ad-5c30-8b98-64e9b859f1c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0f605aaa-ae26-5b68-87f9-17830de80cc0" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=e^{x - y} \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3})&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\cos(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3})&=- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22953525-b5e9-5596-915e-71172f1ce1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705600} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4045a8-553f-5ddb-8fdc-b24d25e31c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705604} {:answer/id #uuid "43532700-ce0a-587a-b915-30e3a1344bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{- \\sqrt{3} - 1}{2 \\sqrt{\\sqrt{3} + 2}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{3}}{2 \\sqrt{\\sqrt{3} + 2}}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705607} {:answer/id #uuid "9f357cc3-0848-5792-a1af-35ba171ed94c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705619} {:answer/id #uuid "e156e8c6-c841-5711-b70b-74566b9eab91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5 - 2 \\sqrt{3}}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{5 - 2 \\sqrt{3}}}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705619} :answer-field/id #uuid "65058df1-dcfa-55c1-ad91-62c3cc090ce9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6f130794-d992-58c1-b0b9-d32d6518a6a8" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=- e^{x - y} \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19092c6c-5acb-5a6e-b36a-167b24277fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705599} {:answer/id #uuid "283c4205-bea2-5e7b-bdea-845d19046ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{- \\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{2 \\sqrt{3} + 7}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2 \\sqrt{3} + 7}}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705601} {:answer/id #uuid "3a64baaf-a95b-5761-8212-1f2e2cc46853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705603} {:answer/id #uuid "433a8fd6-e06d-5391-8b89-a37a47532cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705606} {:answer/id #uuid "e0131dea-e20f-5bf6-97d2-5ec2716e1ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705606} :answer-field/id #uuid "5621bd7c-b2bc-5294-8a53-5152e043a0ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7a8d4ef2-c82d-5f34-ae79-41fc3ab1f3b1" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=- e^{- x - y} \\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)} - 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x - y} \\\\ \\sin(- \\frac{\\pi}{6})&=- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos(- \\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2 e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "402f08ef-6731-5bb7-ad99-bfbb7abaec6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705605} {:answer/id #uuid "529ed544-2fff-5578-af21-3d18cded24f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705608} {:answer/id #uuid "56b518bb-6798-5e44-9c17-8a1a9304c8ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{5 - 2 \\sqrt{3}}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5 - 2 \\sqrt{3}}}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705610} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2642ea-6d46-51ad-b2bb-552f01441cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705623} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd96dc8-6a04-5713-9445-0569db236766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{- \\sqrt{3} - 1}{2 \\sqrt{\\sqrt{3} + 2}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{- \\sqrt{3} - 1}{2 \\sqrt{\\sqrt{3} + 2}}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705605} :answer-field/id #uuid "610b4e73-e3eb-509f-bcaf-1ccad591e9dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8a9ba066-e207-534b-b904-793028ce8309" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=- e^{- x + y} \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x + y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x + y} \\\\ \\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2 e^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5ddfdc-2def-5b02-9885-2175fbe48a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5 - 2 \\sqrt{3}}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{5 - 2 \\sqrt{3}}}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705595} {:answer/id #uuid "163e7732-e3e3-5b62-9a4a-abfcd56c8d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705598} {:answer/id #uuid "28b09933-b2a2-5c7a-bc45-b988e00ccdae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{3}}{2 \\sqrt{\\sqrt{3} + 2}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{3}}{2 \\sqrt{\\sqrt{3} + 2}}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705602} {:answer/id #uuid "af2b1cf4-5c2f-54f2-804f-70f6aa1d8b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705621} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c96a30-9308-5de8-9e1c-5a8a7d432f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2 \\sqrt{3} + 7}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2 \\sqrt{3} + 7}}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705621} :answer-field/id #uuid "85f8cdee-b364-5121-bf7f-b22daff15286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d8fb2de5-609a-50c8-9118-5b8a81628b43" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=e^{x - y} \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{3})&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{3})&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190ab71d-9a59-5922-beaa-496b0d931eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{26}}{26}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705635} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd0a3a6-c5d7-5cd8-9104-b2cada343d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705637} {:answer/id #uuid "6471c28c-d5b1-5500-b147-38a743fc0976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705640} {:answer/id #uuid "a9edb17f-b8b4-58c5-a9ca-4a096a57c05a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705646} {:answer/id #uuid "bb96fb49-6008-5b76-b715-7bf7d8411cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{26}}{26}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705649}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705640} :answer-field/id #uuid "d17b0a12-a0cb-55db-a64d-6d228c674070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "009c76a9-19b6-5987-bc08-66fe79f2c568" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - x y + 2 x - 2 y^{2} - 2 y + 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=- 4 x - y + 2 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- x - 4 y - 2 \\\\ \\nabla f(1,-1)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(1,-1)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(-1\\right)^2+\\left(1\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(1,-1)}{\\|\\nabla f(1,-1)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(-1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1\\right)\\,j}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08673440-e2f5-52de-9775-9136e935d547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{13}}{13}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{13}}{13}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705632} {:answer/id #uuid "11920347-b8cf-5a6f-b76f-26233d2ed184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705634} {:answer/id #uuid "2179ee5d-90fa-5a53-8922-c944dbdbaa30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{85}}{85}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{85}}{85}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705636} {:answer/id #uuid "e274e0db-ef11-5f3c-ac0a-321cc5af4c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{29}}{29}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705654} {:answer/id #uuid "fdba660c-21c8-5d26-9c3a-6b4dee4e0692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{10 \\sqrt{101}}{101}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{101}}{101}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705636} :answer-field/id #uuid "7df7ef12-1283-5635-ba6d-cd7441040f98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "044f8457-c0fd-5eab-8722-bab400b519fd" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - x y + x + 2 y^{2} + y + 3$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=- 4 x - y + 1 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- x + 4 y + 1 \\\\ \\nabla f(-1,-2)&=\\left(7\\right)\\,i+\\left(-6\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(-1,-2)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(7\\right)^2+\\left(-6\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{85}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(-1,-2)}{\\|\\nabla f(-1,-2)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(7\\right)\\,i+\\left(-6\\right)\\,j}{\\sqrt{85}} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{85}}{85}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{85}}{85}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "408abbc4-91a2-50ba-8f3f-8adca5c79b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705638} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce9c874-bf80-53db-aaf5-732e9a4a643d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705642} {:answer/id #uuid "ce30c272-fc57-5723-845c-844b9476f2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{17}}{17}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705652} {:answer/id #uuid "e7325b53-a578-5b8b-aa4d-5ac85ace32de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705655} {:answer/id #uuid "fd65ac56-a466-51e3-9478-5a8d1f676b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{9 \\sqrt{97}}{97}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{97}}{97}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705652} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f46386d-20c5-517b-a2ff-b2ea3585a2bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64aec065-834e-50fe-bd79-786886f778fb" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 2 x y + x + 2 y^{2} - 2 y + 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=4 x + 2 y + 1 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=2 x + 4 y - 2 \\\\ \\nabla f(-1,2)&=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(4\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(-1,2)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(1\\right)^2+\\left(4\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(-1,2)}{\\|\\nabla f(-1,2)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(4\\right)\\,j}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{17}}{17}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42199e28-76ac-595b-bbea-a14be16b4c7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{29}}{29}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705639} {:answer/id #uuid "b03489d7-ff65-5f5f-9879-7aebccad8b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705647} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fcff6a-4247-5870-9431-433075098afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705648} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9e1b32-edb9-53bb-bed5-2349833b0916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{65}}{65}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{7 \\sqrt{65}}{65}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705650} {:answer/id #uuid "be331e77-5dfb-5495-b802-838d1b91073b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705651} :answer-field/id #uuid "08e1699f-b46d-517b-8d42-6e429bd392ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70de0555-31cd-56c7-8976-83b032f9783c" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x y + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)} - 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(5,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Here the logarithm has positive argument $1$, so the function is differentiable near the point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=y + \\frac{1}{x + 2 y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=x + \\frac{2}{x + 2 y} \\\\ \\nabla f(5,-2)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\,i+\\left(7\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(5,-2)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(-1\\right)^2+\\left(7\\right)^2} \\\\ &=5 \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(5,-2)}{\\|\\nabla f(5,-2)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(-1\\right)\\,i+\\left(7\\right)\\,j}{5 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01abf44d-c84d-5754-934c-f463bbfac346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} + 1)\\mathbf i+(- \\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} - 1)\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705859} {:answer/id #uuid "62212ad3-e9bf-5eff-a723-76777309f81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)})\\mathbf i- \\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)}\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705869} {:answer/id #uuid "70185aaf-93bb-5cb4-9a0f-eee672e4e062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705873} {:answer/id #uuid "735b70ef-42fc-58bf-b410-b0fd4e3cf05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2 \\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} + 1)\\mathbf i+(- 2 \\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} - 1)\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705874} {:answer/id #uuid "ea90dea1-e1ab-576c-8c3b-0457241e4aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} - 1)\\mathbf i+(\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} + 1)\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705859} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d6eb33f-2492-5ae0-9f05-2238e5413af8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2674c5a6-fc6f-5b93-85b1-8cbf5b6b1c9d" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=x - y - 2, \\\\ \\nabla f&=\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j \\\\ g(x,y)&=\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)}, \\\\ \\nabla g&=\\frac{1}{x - y - 2}\\mathbf i- \\frac{1}{x - y - 2}\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork where $x - y - 2 > 0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the **product rule**: $\\nabla(fg)=g\\nabla f+f\\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.\n\nNow collect each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\\\ &=(\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)})(1) \\\\ &\\quad+(x - y - 2)\\left(\\frac{1}{x - y - 2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad+(1) \\\\ &=\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\\\ &=(\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)})(-1) \\\\ &\\quad+(x - y - 2)\\left(- \\frac{1}{x - y - 2}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad+(-1) \\\\ &=- \\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient is\n\n$$\n(\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} + 1)\\mathbf i+(- \\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} - 1)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nCollect the contributions to each coordinate separately."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63a68935-b81a-5737-ab11-f518bad10728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\mathbf i+4\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705870} {:answer/id #uuid "76446476-8ee2-5747-8669-dbc231b32078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&- \\frac{3 x + 4 z}{x}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}- \\frac{2 \\left(x + 2 z\\right)}{y}\\mathbf j \\\\ &{}- \\frac{2 \\left(x + 3 z\\right)}{z}\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705876} {:answer/id #uuid "8169e37c-ec11-58ff-893b-4aa9ea7689a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705878} {:answer/id #uuid "b84460f5-c339-58fb-90f6-41ae61fc5f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{3 x + 4 z}{x}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\frac{2 \\left(x + 2 z\\right)}{y}\\mathbf j \\\\ &{}+\\frac{2 \\left(x + 3 z\\right)}{z}\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705884} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f4afda-1fc3-5f36-a332-4bf33e534ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&- \\frac{2 \\left(x + 2 z\\right)}{x}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}- \\frac{2 \\left(x + 2 z\\right)}{y}\\mathbf j \\\\ &{}- \\frac{2 \\left(x + 2 z\\right)}{z}\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705893}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705878} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc0a6192-10f6-5dda-8cbd-fb665dab74fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f49e71d-dc8d-5241-bd3a-061e085fe5b2" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y,z)g(x,y,z)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y,z)&=x^{2} y z + 2 x y z^{2}, \\\\ \\nabla f&=\\begin{aligned}&(2 x y z + 2 y z^{2})\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+(x^{2} z + 2 x z^{2})\\mathbf j \\\\ &{}+(x^{2} y + 4 x y z)\\mathbf k\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{x y z}, \\\\ \\nabla g&=\\begin{aligned}&- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y z}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}- \\frac{1}{x y^{2} z}\\mathbf j \\\\ &{}- \\frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssume $x\\ne0$, $y\\ne0$, $z\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the **product rule**: $\\nabla(fg)=g\\nabla f+f\\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.\n\nNow collect each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)(2 x y z + 2 y z^{2}) \\\\ &\\quad+(x^{2} y z + 2 x y z^{2})\\left(- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y z}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\left(x + z\\right)}{x} \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- \\frac{x + 2 z}{x}\\right) \\\\ &=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)(x^{2} z + 2 x z^{2}) \\\\ &\\quad+(x^{2} y z + 2 x y z^{2})\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y^{2} z}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{x + 2 z}{y} \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- \\frac{x + 2 z}{y}\\right) \\\\ &=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{z}&=g f_{z}+f g_{z} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)(x^{2} y + 4 x y z) \\\\ &\\quad+(x^{2} y z + 2 x y z^{2})\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{x + 4 z}{z} \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- \\frac{x + 2 z}{z}\\right) \\\\ &=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nCollect the contributions to each coordinate separately."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b8d9371-bf67-529e-933e-70f94738d179" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&- y \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}- x \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705861} {:answer/id #uuid "6902e327-0368-5a8c-b872-23faf504f0b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&y \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+x \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705871} {:answer/id #uuid "69e9079e-f795-58e9-81c1-5a93d205a527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\mathbf i+x\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705872} {:answer/id #uuid "79856485-ed2a-5aed-8fb5-adcc8faa5053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y \\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)}\\mathbf i+x \\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)}\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705877} {:answer/id #uuid "88d5ef21-d05d-51ec-8176-9694e81b1bb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&y \\left(2 \\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+x \\left(2 \\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705871} :answer-field/id #uuid "d304936f-00a7-592b-8b98-a1ff654d7663" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "47c71fb8-632c-5b91-bb4c-8723252adf5e" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=x y, \\\\ \\nabla f&=y\\mathbf i+x\\mathbf j \\\\ g(x,y)&=\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)}, \\\\ \\nabla g&=\\frac{1}{x}\\mathbf i+\\frac{1}{y}\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork where $2 x y > 0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the **product rule**: $\\nabla(fg)=g\\nabla f+f\\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.\n\nNow collect each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\\\ &=(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)})(y) \\\\ &\\quad+(x y)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\ &=y \\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad+(y) \\\\ &=y \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\\\ &=(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)})(x) \\\\ &\\quad+(x y)\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right) \\\\ &=x \\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad+(x) \\\\ &=x \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&y \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+x \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the contributions to each coordinate separately."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e099b2-1c11-5d0e-a74a-ba5e1881d6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{4 \\left(2 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} + y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\frac{2 \\left(- 2 x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705862} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb8dae2-129d-5a09-b4f1-ac3faf9aa29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{4 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\frac{y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705864} {:answer/id #uuid "31442dbe-31b7-52e0-8935-1a29bdc7241e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{2 \\left(4 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} - y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\frac{2 \\left(x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705865} {:answer/id #uuid "74a6e24c-ca10-5ba8-82b3-f66ed2bd70d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{2 \\left(2 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} + y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\frac{- 2 x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705875} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb196cf-e98b-5801-b008-67288bd48e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{4 \\left(- x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} + y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}- \\frac{4 x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705887}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705875} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b25d21-a333-5a94-b45a-4cb6160fff11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2cbd5c6-85bc-5dd6-8e34-a08261f0b986" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}, \\\\ \\nabla f&=\\frac{2 y}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\\mathbf i- \\frac{2 x}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\\mathbf j \\\\ g(x,y)&=\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}, \\\\ \\nabla g&=\\frac{4 x}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssume $y\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the **product rule**: $\\nabla(fg)=g\\nabla f+f\\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.\n\nNow collect each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\\\ &=(\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}})\\left(\\frac{2 y}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad+(\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)})\\left(\\frac{4 x}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 y}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}} \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\frac{4 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\left(2 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} + y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\\\ &=(\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}})\\left(- \\frac{2 x}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad+(\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)})\\left(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 x}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}} \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\frac{y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{- 2 x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{2 \\left(2 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} + y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\frac{- 2 x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the contributions to each coordinate separately."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b63376f-33e3-5009-8d57-fe234141dbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 4 x y^{2} \\left(2 x^{2} + y\\right)\\mathbf i- 2 x^{2} y \\left(2 x^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705866} {:answer/id #uuid "912c026b-0368-5121-83ad-060b32058a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 x y^{3}\\mathbf i+2 x^{2} y \\left(2 x^{2} + y\\right)\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705881} {:answer/id #uuid "9d906686-dfa1-502b-b5a1-5babff5f74be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 8 x^{3} y^{2}\\mathbf i- 4 x^{2} y^{2}\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705882} {:answer/id #uuid "b003cbb3-ce36-5d1f-8bc0-b0cf05a20ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 x y^{2} \\left(2 x^{2} + y\\right)\\mathbf i+2 x^{2} y \\left(2 x^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705883} {:answer/id #uuid "f42f39f0-c3d0-567b-bdbe-3945207f4bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 4 x^{3} y^{2}\\mathbf i- 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705892}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705866} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ec6f8c-033e-5248-997b-1ecc4ff533e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "deba76c4-81f3-596c-bf03-cea2f310fcb1" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=- x^{2} - y, \\\\ \\nabla f&=- 2 x\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j \\\\ g(x,y)&=2 x^{2} y^{2}, \\\\ \\nabla g&=4 x y^{2}\\mathbf i+4 x^{2} y\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the **product rule**: $\\nabla(fg)=g\\nabla f+f\\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.\n\nNow collect each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\\\ &=(2 x^{2} y^{2})(- 2 x) \\\\ &\\quad+(- x^{2} - y)(4 x y^{2}) \\\\ &=- 4 x^{3} y^{2} \\\\ &\\quad+(- 4 x y^{2} \\left(x^{2} + y\\right)) \\\\ &=- 4 x y^{2} \\left(2 x^{2} + y\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\\\ &=(2 x^{2} y^{2})(-1) \\\\ &\\quad+(- x^{2} - y)(4 x^{2} y) \\\\ &=- 2 x^{2} y^{2} \\\\ &\\quad+(- 4 x^{2} y \\left(x^{2} + y\\right)) \\\\ &=- 2 x^{2} y \\left(2 x^{2} + 3 y\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient is\n\n$$\n- 4 x y^{2} \\left(2 x^{2} + y\\right)\\mathbf i- 2 x^{2} y \\left(2 x^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nCollect the contributions to each coordinate separately."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f7a428-e4b9-5cf7-804e-80af926f4040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=1\\\\y&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + 2\\\\z&=e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705924} {:answer/id #uuid "2305897b-feda-5ab8-a666-9b65a8bcd973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=1\\\\y&=2\\\\z&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705931} {:answer/id #uuid "7c431659-9a3d-52c9-81ae-36e8c598ae19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + 1\\\\y&=2\\\\z&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705944} {:answer/id #uuid "93965054-0281-5e8a-92d4-b1c228b63a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + 1\\\\y&=2\\\\z&=e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705947} {:answer/id #uuid "f071dfe1-3d71-5e03-892a-0812240a324f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + 1\\\\y&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + 2\\\\z&=e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705953}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705931} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab6e744-82e8-5f3b-8cb6-459842390076" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "117c2134-e2a8-5d37-bce5-c21f4883b2f8" :question/problem "Find parametric equations of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ln{\\left(\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2} \\right)}\\\\\n&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(1,2,e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=\\ln{\\left(\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2} \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=1$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{2 \\left(x - 1\\right)}{\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{2 \\left(y - 2\\right)}{\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{2 \\left(z - 2\\right)}{\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(y - 2\\right)&=0\\\\\n\\left(x - 1\\right)&=0\\\\\n\\left(z - 2\\right)&=e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=\\frac{2 \\left(0\\right)}{\\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=\\frac{2 \\left(0\\right)}{\\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=\\frac{2 \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)}{\\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(1,2,e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2)=\\langle 0,0,\\frac{2}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nMultiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $\\frac{1}{2}$ to obtain the equivalent direction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=\\frac{1}{2}\\langle 0,0,\\frac{2}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}\\rangle=\\langle 0,0,e^{- \\frac{1}{2}}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,2,e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 0,0,e^{- \\frac{1}{2}}\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nEquating coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=1\\\\y&=2\\\\z&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "761ff216-38ad-5070-8bc5-1b42ff56f2bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle -1,0,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 0,-16,-8\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705942} {:answer/id #uuid "87cab669-5756-59b7-8ea3-1ed38b9dc171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle -1,0,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 0,-16,0\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705945} {:answer/id #uuid "db94bf15-1057-5e35-8124-4df7d8429d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle -1,0,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 0,-16,-4\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705951} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b51d18-1abe-5d95-af4a-9b152af46c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle -1,0,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 0,-16,8\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705952} {:answer/id #uuid "fa961a15-4de4-5065-bc2f-6283e8318464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle -1,0,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 0,-16,4\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705955} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8203691-2fcb-5b27-b473-43f02569cd2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4f7afc84-952e-5e4b-96a6-515daaac25fb" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&-4 \\left(- x - 1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+4 \\left(1 - y\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=12\\\\\n&{}+\\left(z + 1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-6 \\left(z + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(-1,0,1)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=-\\left(z + 1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-4 \\left(- x - 1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+4 \\left(1 - y\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+6 \\left(z + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=12$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=8 \\left(- x - 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=-8 \\left(1 - y\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=6\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(z + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(z + 1\\right)&=2\\\\\n\\left(- x - 1\\right)&=0\\\\\n\\left(1 - y\\right)&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=8 \\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=-8 \\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=6\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(-1,0,1)=\\langle 0,-8,2\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nMultiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $2$ to obtain the equivalent direction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=2\\langle 0,-8,2\\rangle=\\langle 0,-16,4\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle -1,0,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 0,-16,4\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079d2197-2dc8-5ae0-92fd-3f51fe1a8344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},1,-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705923} {:answer/id #uuid "2db4ac74-b16f-58a7-a971-8f8d0742b200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705934} {:answer/id #uuid "3c18f78a-eb67-5bab-83c2-c6bc2398d3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705936} {:answer/id #uuid "493e584a-9cac-5253-ae91-5754e08d4eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},0,-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705938} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0d46fe-50b2-5c2f-b5dc-8c4aff625202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},-1,-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705934} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ca93aa0-504e-5847-b36f-29319e32416c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5b821f7d-0519-503a-8177-317da9e0701d" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&\\left(y - 2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(- x - 2\\right)\\\\\n&{}+2 \\left(- x - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(- \\frac{5}{2},5,0)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=\\left(y - 2\\right)\\\\\n&{}-\\left(- x - 2\\right)\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(- x - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=0$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=1\\\\\n&{}+4 \\left(- x - 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=-4 \\left(z + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(- x - 2\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\n\\left(z + 1\\right)&=1\\\\\n\\left(y - 2\\right)&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=1\\\\\n&{}+4 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=1\\\\\n&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=-4 \\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(- \\frac{5}{2},5,0)=\\langle 3,1,-4\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nMultiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $\\frac{1}{2}$ to obtain the equivalent direction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=\\frac{1}{2}\\langle 3,1,-4\\rangle=\\langle \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},-2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},-2\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e179bb0-99e0-5b4a-be18-5343ddcd602a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 3,-4,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 2,5,3\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705926} {:answer/id #uuid "1535a846-b0ac-5722-946a-7ba37666039e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 3,-4,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle -2,-10,3\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705928} {:answer/id #uuid "16bd5be3-8e81-5c54-8dd8-a25b0588a15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 3,-4,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle -2,-5,3\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705929} {:answer/id #uuid "404fb0e0-e07b-5dcd-8f73-5dfd849de015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 3,-4,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle -2,5,3\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705937} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2494f7-3475-5caf-a954-ef4d4f5e63aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 3,-4,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 2,-5,3\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705941} :answer-field/id #uuid "b66acd86-22a3-5c69-9315-204fc37d4df9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb356ead-9c76-5b6c-8135-36503fe687eb" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&\\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(- y - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} \\left(- z\\right)\\\\\n&{}+\\left(- z\\right)^{2} \\left(- y - 2\\right)\\\\\n&=5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(3,-4,1)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=\\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(- y - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} \\left(- z\\right)\\\\\n&{}+\\left(- z\\right)^{2} \\left(- y - 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=5$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\left(- y - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+2 \\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(- z\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=-\\left(- z\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(- y - 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=-\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(- z\\right) \\left(- y - 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(x - 2\\right)&=1\\\\\n\\left(- z\\right)&=-1\\\\\n\\left(- y - 2\\right)&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=\\left(2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(-1\\right)\\\\\n&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=-\\left(-1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=-\\left(1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(-1\\right) \\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(3,-4,1)=\\langle 2,-5,3\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 3,-4,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 2,-5,3\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f24dd21-13b7-5c00-9363-ad415328a6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},-1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705930} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfc4d03-e143-5cc4-b9bf-35a1ea97a3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},- \\frac{1}{2}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705933} {:answer/id #uuid "2faa4958-e3ce-5f03-a110-35a2c12d0846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705935} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc52d61-7f78-5bc3-8bb0-db3e2a26165e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},0\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705946} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3d335d-5b9c-5bd1-bdc8-ab2e421959fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705935} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c37b48c-76ce-5f9c-b13d-e9f3e5da6d74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "faa8cbcc-cc2d-5db3-9bae-d21e162768f5" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ln{\\left(\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z \\right)}\\\\\n&=-\\left(- y - 2\\right) x z\\\\\n&{}+\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(0,-3,1)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=\\left(- y - 2\\right) x z\\\\\n&{}+\\ln{\\left(\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(- y - 2\\right) z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z}\\\\\n&{}-x z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(- y - 2\\right) x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(- y - 2\\right)&=1\\\\\nx&=0\\\\\nz&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=\\frac{1}{\\left(1\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(1\\right)}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(1\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(1\\right)}\\\\\n&{}-\\left(0\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=\\frac{1}{\\left(1\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(1\\right)}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(1\\right) \\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(0,-3,1)=\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29444a50-2016-5112-a7aa-349d1e3f2475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 x - 3 y - 13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705967} {:answer/id #uuid "2d35f92e-2524-5dfc-8919-7da771d78c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x + 2 y + 4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705968} {:answer/id #uuid "37ad6b12-a139-51be-9405-4efa9d171631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6 x - y - 15=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705970} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf2e01a-bf6f-51ff-a481-71ae2c0677e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x + y + 1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705990} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f0d5c8-1a85-5451-9814-42725b4e3415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 x - y - 7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705992} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ca22918-6a40-5290-a71b-a6f2fbdfc9ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09ef73ce-e78d-5b57-bcac-17f8db7ee359" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2 x^{2} + 4 x \\left(y + 2\\right) - x - 2 y - 4=x + 2 y + 4.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=2 x^{2} + 4 x \\left(y + 2\\right) - 2 x - 4 y - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the curve gives the level set $f(x,y)=0.$ Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other coordinate fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=4 x + 4 y + 6 \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=4 x - 4 \\\\\n\\nabla f(2,-3)&=\\left\\langle 2,4\\right\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it gives a direction for the normal line. A line through the given point parallel to $\\left\\langle 2,4\\right\\rangle$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(x-x_0)&=a(y-y_0) \\\\\n\\left(4\\right)\\left(x - 2\\right)&=\\left(2\\right)\\left(y + 3\\right) \\\\\n4 x - 2 y - 14&=0 \\\\\n2 x - y - 7&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the whole equation gives the normal line $2 x - y - 7=0.$ Its direction is parallel to the gradient; using the gradient as the coefficient vector would give the tangent line instead."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39d64891-e0b0-5d25-9532-a8fab6ce24b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y - 1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705971} {:answer/id #uuid "7274a280-4e57-5873-9615-b2fc28f27b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x + 4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705981} {:answer/id #uuid "815f7926-e2bf-536b-a0d1-f3de21c7f17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705983} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5aceb7-906d-565b-b0f2-4843756249e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705985} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b9eef5-854d-5519-92dc-a8842adaf41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x + y + 3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705986}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705971} :answer-field/id #uuid "578984c7-7a0a-5098-89c4-154c0523595f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a3fbf27-acfc-5eb3-b330-20d61fbdbd03" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\left(x + 2\\right) \\left(y - 2\\right) - 8=2 x + \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} - 3.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(-4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=-3+\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&{}+\\left(x + 2\\right)^{2}+2 x \\\\\n&{}- \\left(x + 2\\right) \\left(y - 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the curve gives the level set $f(x,y)=-8.$ Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other coordinate fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2 x - y + 8 \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=- x + 2 y - 6 \\\\\n\\nabla f(-4,1)&=\\left\\langle -1,0\\right\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it gives a direction for the normal line. A line through the given point parallel to $\\left\\langle -1,0\\right\\rangle$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(x-x_0)&=a(y-y_0) \\\\\n\\left(0\\right)\\left(x + 4\\right)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\left(y - 1\\right) \\\\\ny - 1&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the whole equation gives the normal line $y - 1=0.$ This is a horizontal line. The direction relation avoids division by a zero gradient component."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e8b31a-6d95-5aec-9d33-ae67533259ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7 x - 3 y + 17=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705964} {:answer/id #uuid "201ceb56-ffd9-5391-9774-7d9617253401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 x + 7 y - 1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705966} {:answer/id #uuid "537ec0ec-ad33-5e28-9470-fba693a3060f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6 x + 7 y + 5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705975} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d38d08-14e1-510d-bd5c-24061d23b28d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 x - 7 y + 13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705987} {:answer/id #uuid "d0da5dc9-2dc1-595b-bf51-93a51dc6b8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12 x + 7 y + 17=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705966} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce5977f-e7a5-5469-8e58-f0fdc63aba39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3656ccb8-4ea1-53ce-aa2a-0c05207c5100" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&-2+\\left(y + 1\\right)^{2}- 2 y+3 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&=11+4 \\left(x + 1\\right) \\left(y + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=-2+\\left(y + 1\\right)^{2}- 2 y \\\\\n&{}+3 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&{}- 4 \\left(x + 1\\right) \\left(y + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the curve gives the level set $f(x,y)=11.$ Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other coordinate fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=6 x - 4 y + 2 \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=- 4 x + 2 y - 4 \\\\\n\\nabla f(-2,1)&=\\left\\langle -14,6\\right\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it gives a direction for the normal line. A line through the given point parallel to $\\left\\langle -14,6\\right\\rangle$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(x-x_0)&=a(y-y_0) \\\\\n\\left(6\\right)\\left(x + 2\\right)&=\\left(-14\\right)\\left(y - 1\\right) \\\\\n6 x + 14 y - 2&=0 \\\\\n3 x + 7 y - 1&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the whole equation gives the normal line $3 x + 7 y - 1=0.$ Its direction is parallel to the gradient; using the gradient as the coefficient vector would give the tangent line instead."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e56b09b-3e2f-52d8-8cb4-3ba4d11b603b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8 x - 5 y - 12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705962} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8db3bf-e310-5b12-a314-403338f57db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8 x + 5 y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705973} {:answer/id #uuid "51d3e9c4-058e-52e2-993d-5ea9a961af37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5 x - 8 y - 27=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705974} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbfc727-7dce-55cb-b668-c9922c48f8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5 x - 8 y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705977} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ed5ef0-1c25-5dee-bb9f-6f60f2823524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8 x + 5 y + 28=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705989} :answer-field/id #uuid "10c3bb0f-62d1-5de9-b217-97b1065ef115" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60cc9620-5a35-5993-8749-d08127d77bb2" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $6 x \\left(y + 2\\right) + 13=x^{2} - 2 y + 5 \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2} - 4.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(-1,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=-4+x^{2}- 2 y+5 \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&{}- 6 x \\left(y + 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the curve gives the level set $f(x,y)=13.$ Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other coordinate fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2 x - 6 y - 12 \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=- 6 x + 10 y + 18 \\\\\n\\nabla f(-1,-4)&=\\left\\langle 10,-16\\right\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it gives a direction for the normal line. A line through the given point parallel to $\\left\\langle 10,-16\\right\\rangle$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(x-x_0)&=a(y-y_0) \\\\\n\\left(-16\\right)\\left(x + 1\\right)&=\\left(10\\right)\\left(y + 4\\right) \\\\\n- 16 x - 10 y - 56&=0 \\\\\n8 x + 5 y + 28&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the whole equation gives the normal line $8 x + 5 y + 28=0.$ Its direction is parallel to the gradient; using the gradient as the coefficient vector would give the tangent line instead."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be90080-bfd2-5823-b838-e58f722ed27c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 x + y + 3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705961} {:answer/id #uuid "192c98c0-ffa1-57f9-97d0-86f683e64c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x - 3 y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705965} {:answer/id #uuid "7951e922-5543-52a8-97ea-da901a5daee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 x + y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705982} {:answer/id #uuid "dee3ce44-c435-5532-a63b-83750886401c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x - 3 y - 9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705991} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7d573d-7f4a-5f83-96ef-ba0935753683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x + 3 y + 9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705993}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705991} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a111891-a36a-5c34-8220-87257d945d7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9bd8fdfc-a6ab-5f59-8c13-7258dcfe084c" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2 x + y + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{3} \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} - 1=2 x + y - 5.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\left(x - 1\\right)^{3} \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the curve gives the level set $f(x,y)=-4.$ Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other coordinate fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=3 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(x - 1\\right)^{3} \\left(2 y + 2\\right) \\\\\n\\nabla f(0,-3)&=\\left\\langle 12,4\\right\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it gives a direction for the normal line. A line through the given point parallel to $\\left\\langle 12,4\\right\\rangle$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(x-x_0)&=a(y-y_0) \\\\\n\\left(4\\right)\\left(x\\right)&=\\left(12\\right)\\left(y + 3\\right) \\\\\n4 x - 12 y - 36&=0 \\\\\nx - 3 y - 9&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the whole equation gives the normal line $x - 3 y - 9=0.$ Its direction is parallel to the gradient; using the gradient as the coefficient vector would give the tangent line instead."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16fa4f2a-6b53-51dd-b92e-03de2382ff60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{e}\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j-\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706205} {:answer/id #uuid "435b136a-e8d3-5817-9a31-8e673c91ec15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706212} {:answer/id #uuid "5ddf85ba-75e3-56f9-8bb6-a3e8b45fb2e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-e^{-1}\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(1 - e^{-1}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(1 - e^{-1}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706219} {:answer/id #uuid "d80b1942-18ba-5253-8302-3701d57122d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{e}\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j-\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706233} {:answer/id #uuid "f426f094-e721-595f-9919-f747a3d4a41e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(1 - e^{-1}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+e^{-1}\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(1 - e^{-1}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706212} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc02724a-9856-58b3-bd7d-c6596de98324" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4d8332ff-4123-5303-9015-bf53f0020c7c" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x e^{z} + y e^{x} + z e^{y}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(-1,0,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=y e^{x} + e^{z}\\\\f_y&=z e^{y} + e^{x}\\\\f_z&=x e^{z} + e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(-1,0,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(P)&=\\left(0\\right) e^{\\left(-1\\right)} + e^{\\left(-1\\right)}=e^{-1}\\\\f_y(P)&=\\left(-1\\right) e^{\\left(0\\right)} + e^{\\left(-1\\right)}=-1 + e^{-1}\\\\f_z(P)&=\\left(-1\\right) e^{\\left(-1\\right)} + e^{\\left(0\\right)}=1 - e^{-1}\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=e^{-1}\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(1 - e^{-1}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(1 - e^{-1}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n2\\nabla f(P)=\\frac{2}{e}\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $\\frac{2}{e}\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}]}
